ion they aren't even screened for  an eating disorder  despite time and time again research  showing that these things are heavily  correlated that is a problem so we don't  actually know whether or not it's  measuring anything that is different  than an eating disorder meanwhile one  side that used the Yale Addiction scale  found that a persistent desire or  repeated and unsuccessful attempts to  quit a particular food was the only  characteristic of food addiction that  was commonly found in other words the  other common characteristics of  addiction were not common in relation to  food even if this tool is not perfect  there are tons of other reasons why this  concept of food addiction prevails so  let's dig into the  okay so let's talk about first of all  the dopamine response to food now  dopamine is one of the major  neurotransmitters that are released from  the brain when somebody does drugs  so from smoking a cigarette to injecting  heroin dopamine levels rise one of the  big arguments for the idea that food is  an addictive substance is based on  research that have shown that we have a  dopamine response from food that is  similar to our dopamine response from  drugs in a small human study researchers  gave participants either a tasty  milkshake or a tasteless solution and  their brains were analyzed the  researchers have found that in the  participants who had the tasty milkshake  they saw a dopamine response at two  times first when the milkshake was  consumed and second when that food  entered into the stomach so this  dopamine release occurs in the dorsal  stratum part of the brain and it's the  same part of the brain that releases  dopamine when somebody does cocaine now  let's talk about rats now in a very  famous 2005 study three groups of rats  were given a sugar solution one group  was given the sugar solution for 12  hours the other group was given it twice  and the other group was given it ad  libitum which basically means they could  have it whenever they wanted so  researchers then looked at the rats  brains and found that on the first day  all the rats released dopamine now this  is of course to be expected because we  always release dopamine in response to a  new novel food just so that our body can  kind of keep tabs on it in case it turns  out to be like poison or something so  basically your brain is kind of on high  alert to new foods interestingly the  rats were only given that sugar for 12  hours a day binge on that sugar and  their dopamine levels continue to rise  over the course of a month as they  needed to eat more and more and more of  that sugar in those 12 hours the rats  who are given free rein on the sugar did  not demonstrate bench like tendencies  which I'll talk about more later and  after day one their dopamine levels  actually stabilized in other words it  was the binge like tendencies that  produce a dopamine response similar to  what we see with hard drugs next let's  talk about withdrawal symptoms so  another big reason why people really buy  into the idea of food addiction is  because of the research on withdrawal  evidence from grad studies have found  that when rats are used to eating sugar  and then somebody takes that away from  them they experience a lot of withdrawal  like symptoms like anxiety depression  changes in blood pressure and heart rate  when researchers looked at the brains of  these rats experiencing withdrawal they  found that their dopamine levels had  dropped which is also something that we  would see in a person experiencing  withdrawal from hard drugs they also  noted a rise in the neurotransmitter  acetylcholine which is also something  that we would see in people who are  experiencing withdrawal from drugs and  finally let's talk about the behavioral  response when we compare sugar versus  healthy foods the other major argument  about why food and particularly sugar is  unique in its addictive like qualities  comes from that 2005 study that we  already d